{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Elohim spoke.</b> Elohim means nothing but 'judge'; for there are parashot in the Torah where if a person fulfills them he receives reward, and if not, he does not receive punishment for them — one might think the Ten Commandments too are so; therefore Scripture says ״וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים״ — a judge who exacts punishment (Mekhilta)." "<b>All these words.</b> This teaches that the Holy One, blessed be He, spoke the Ten Commandments in a single utterance — something impossible for a human to say. If so, what does Scripture mean by going on to say each one separately, ״אָנֹכִי״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״? That He went back and repeated each commandment individually (ibid.)." "<b>Saying.</b> This teaches that they would answer: for an affirmative — yes; for a negative — no (ibid.)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:14.998155655Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}